"Twist and Shout: Faster memory checking arguments via one-hot addressing and increments (https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/105)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/105\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 76\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2025/105\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†Twist and Shout: Faster memory checking arguments via one-hot addressing and increments】\nL32: \nL33: Srinath Setty, Microsoft Research\nL34: \nL35: Justin Thaler, Georgetown University, a16z crypto research\nL36: \nL37: Michael Zhu, a16z crypto\nL38: ##### Abstract\nL39: Memory checking arguments enable an untrusted prover to prove that it is correctly processing reads and writes to memory. They are used widely in modern SNARKs, especially in zkVMs, where the prover proves the correct execution of a CPU including the correctness of memory operations. We describe a new approach for memory checking, called the method of one-hot addressing and increments. We instantiate this method via two families of protocols, called Twist and Shout.\nL40: Twist supports read/write memories, while Shout targets read-only memories (also known as lookup arguments). Both have logarithmic verifier costs. Unlike prior works, these protocols do not invoke \"grand product\" or \"grand sum\" arguments. Twist and Shout reduce commitment costs in two Jolt settings: 32 RISC-V registers and structured lookup tables of size $2^64$.\nL41: With curve-based commitments, Twist at $d=1$ has about 3.7x fewer nonzero committed values and 1.7x fewer field multiplications than Spice for 32 registers; Shout at $d=2$ has at least 8x fewer nonzero committed values and 3.4x fewer field multiplications than Lasso for structured tables. We integrate Twist and Shout into the Jolt zkVM. They speed up the Jolt prover 3x while reducing proof size by about 5x.\nL42: Note: This update includes several concrete optimizations as well as additional details and corrections regarding one-hot checking in small-characteristic fields.\nL43: ##### Metadata\nL44: \nL45: Available format(s)\nL46:     【17†PDF】\nL47: Category\nL48:     【18†Cryptographic protocols】\nL49: Publication info\nL50:     Preprint.\nL51: Keywords\nL52:     【19†SNARKs】【20†memory-checking arguments】【21†sum-check protocol】【22†zkVMs】\nL53: Contact author(s)\nL54:     srinath @ microsoft com\nL55: justin r thaler @ gmail com\nL56: mzhu @ a16z com\nL57: History\nL58:     2026-10-17: last of 8 revisions\nL59:     2025-01-22: received\nL60:     【23†See all versions】\nL61: Short URL\nL62:     【24†https://ia.cr/2025/105†ia.cr】\nL63: License\nL64: \nL65: 【25†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL66: BibTeX 【26†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL67: \nL68:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2025/105,\nL69:           author = {Srinath Setty and Justin Thaler and Michael Zhu},\nL70:           title = {Twist and Shout: Faster memory checking arguments via one-hot addressing and increments},\nL71:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2025/105},\nL72:           year = {2025},\nL73:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/105}\nL74:     }\nL75: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn3view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/105/20261017:104050\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/105/20261017%3A104050 is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nzkLLMPoT: Efficient Zero Knowledge Proof of Training for Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.08258v2)\nciteturn3view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.08258v2\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 437\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: 【0†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org】 Report Issue 【1†Back to Abstract】 【2†Download PDF】 Contents Reading mode Colour theme\nL13:   1. 【3†Abstract】\nL14:   2. 【4†1 Introduction】\nL15:   3. 【5†2 Preliminaries】\nL16:     1. 【6†2.1 Zero Knowledge Proof】\nL17:     2. 【7†2.2 Threat Model】\nL18:   4. 【8†3 zkLLMPoT】\nL19:     1. 【9†3.1 Formulation】\nL20:     2. 【10†3.2 Protocol Components】\nL21:     3. 【11†3.3 Putting It All Together】\nL22:   5. 【12†4 Experiments】\nL23:     1. 【13†4.1 Experimental Setup】\nL24:     2. 【14†4.2 Implementation】\nL25:     3. 【15†4.3 Main Results】\nL26:     4. 【16†4.4 Operator Efficiency】\nL27:     5. 【17†4.5 Generalization to Broader Model Scales】\nL28:     6. 【18†4.6 Loss Functions and Fine-Tuning】\nL29:   6. 【19†5 Conclusion】\nL30:   7. 【20†References】\nL31:   8. 【21†A Related Work】\nL32:   9. 【22†B Details of the Zero-Knowledge Protocol】\nL33:     1. 【23†B.1 Multilinear extensions】\nL34:     2. 【24†B.2 The sumcheck protocol】\nL35:     3. 【25†B.3 The polynomial commitment scheme】\nL36:     4. 【26†B.4 Reducing the forward pass to sumchecks and openings】\nL37:     5. 【27†B.5 Operator gadgets】\nL38:     6. 【28†B.6 Soundness】\nL39:     7. 【29†B.7 Zero knowledge】\nL40: 【30†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org】\nL41: \nL42: arXiv:2610.08258v2 [cs.AI] 11 Oct 2026\nL43: # zkLLMPoT: Efficient Zero Knowledge Proof of Training for Large Language Models\nL44: Junkai Liang Affiliation:  Peking University Affiliation:  Singapore Management University    Zhanpeng Guo Affiliation:  China Telecom Quantum Information Technology Group Co., Ltd    Pengfei Wu Affiliation:  Peking University Affiliation:  Singapore Management University    Qingni Shen Affiliation:  Peking University    Jiaheng Zhang Affiliation:  National University of Singapore    Zhonghai Wu Affiliation:  Peking University    Haiyang Xue Affiliation:  Singapore Management University    Shengfang Zhai Affiliation:  National University of Singapore\nL45: ###### Abstract\nL46: Auditing the claimed outcomes of large language model (LLM) training is challenging when model weights and training data are private, while cryptographically proving the full training process is prohibitively expensive at Transformer scale. We present zkLLMPoT, a zero-knowledge framework that certifies auditor-defined properties of a trained checkpoint through forward evaluation rather than verification of its optimization trajectory.\nL47: zkLLMPoT includes 2 phases: 1) The trainer fixes the architecture and the model weights are committed. Then the auditor selects challenge sequences, preventing the trainer from modifying the checkpoint in response to the audit data. 2) Then the trainer proves the objective value attained by the committed model on those sequences. This formulation makes the certification cost independent of the number of training iterations, without revealing model weights or requiring access to private training data.\nL48: We build on sumcheck- and lookup-based arguments to certify Transformer computations, while supporting next-token loss and task-specific audit objectives. Across four model families, operator-level benchmarks yield proving times of 41–59 seconds for 1.1–1.5B-parameter models and 131 seconds at 13B for the covered operators, with verification below half a second at a sequence length of 512.\nL49: ## 1 Introduction\nL50: Large language models (LLMs) increasingly serve as critical infrastructure for language understanding, generation, and decision support (【31†Brown et al., 2020】), yet assessing their training outcomes raises pressing privacy and trustworthiness concerns.\nL51: Trained models can memorize and leak sensitive examples (【32†Shokri et al., 2017】; 【33†Carlini et al., 2021】), so institutions may withhold training data and model weights from external auditors, leaving the public with only a model card or an API endpoint.\nL52: Consequently, when a trainer announces a new LLM, users and regulators often cannot independently determine whether the released checkpoint actually satisfies the capability or policy claims attributed to its training (【34†Waiwitlikhit et al., 2024】; 【35†Srivastava et al., 2024】). Without a cryptographic certificate tied to the checkpoint, such claims remain difficult to audit despite their legal, commercial, and safety consequences.\nL53: Existing approaches to trustworthy LLM computation leave this gap largely unresolved. First, a substantial line of security work develops zero-knowledge proofs for inference integrity, ranging from CNN and general neural-network predictors (【36†Liu et al., 2021】; 【37†Weng et al., 2021】; 【38†Hao et al., 2024】; 【39†Chen et al., 2024】) to LLM-oriented systems such as zkLLM and zkGPT (【40†Sun et al., 2024】; 【41†Qu et al., 2025】).\nL54: These systems target per-query serving integrity: they certify that a committed model produced a claimed output, rather than providing a checkpoint-level audit against an objective and challenge set chosen by an auditor after the model is committed. Second, cryptographic proofs of training that do target the learning process typically encode full gradient descent with backpropagation. Even for non-transformer networks this is already expensive.\nL55: For example, Kaizen requires about 15 minutes of proving time per training iteration on a VGG-11 model (【42†Abbaszadeh et al., 2024】), and related certificates such as confidential proofs of differentially private training can take on the order of 100 hours even on CIFAR-10 (【43†Shamsabadi et al., 2024】). At LLM scale, naively proving every backward pass is therefore impractical with current techniques.\nL56: Third, early practical zkPoT protocols are largely restricted to demonstration-level models such as logistic regression (【44†Garg et al., 2023】), while optimistic replication-based verifiable training still assumes a third-party auditor who can re-execute training (【35†Srivastava et al., 2024】; 【34†Waiwitlikhit et al., 2024】).\nL57: Taken together, prior work either targets per-query inference, proves the training trajectory through an expensive backpropagation circuit, or scales only to demonstration-level learners. Efficient checkpoint-level, commit-then-challenge auditing for LLM training outcomes therefore remains largely open.\nL58: In this work, we present zkLLMPoT, a zero-knowledge framework for certifying properties of a checkpoint that are claimed as outcomes of LLM training or fine-tuning. Figure 【45†1】 shows the audit workflow. In the full protocol, zkLLMPoT lets a trainer prove that a committed checkpoint attains a declared objective on auditor-chosen data without exposing the weights or making the private training corpus part of the proved relation.\nL59: The same interface can express a declared group-fairness metric or a per-example loss used as a memorization and membership-risk signal (【34†Waiwitlikhit et al., 2024】). Built on zk-SNARKs, the proof size and verification time are independent of the number of training iterations and do not require re-executing training. Our prototype measures the principal Transformer operators needed by this relation and provides full circuit composition and zero-knowledge blinding.\nL60: Figure 1: Audit workflow of zkLLMPoT. After publishing public auxiliary information, the trainer commits a checkpoint. The auditor then issues challenge sequences under a chosen objective (next-token loss, task prompts, or a safety check). The auditor verifies a zero-knowledge proof and accepts or rejects the claimed outcome without learning the private training data or model weights.\nL61: Our protocol attests to properties of a committed checkpoint of a large language model. It can show that (i) the checkpoint improves over a committed reference state on auditor-chosen sequences; (ii) it attains a declared capability on domain-specific tasks; and (iii) the evaluation follows the declared Transformer architecture and objective. The zero-knowledge protocol hides the weights and intermediate activations. Achieving such a certificate raises two challenges.\nL62: First, proving the training process itself is prohibitively slow: every step adds a forward pass, a backward pass, and an optimizer update to the proved relation, so the proving time grows with the length of the training run. Second, if the evaluation data are known before the checkpoint is fixed, the trainer can overfit the checkpoint to those data, and a good result on them no longer reflects the claimed training outcome.\nL63: To make the proof practical, zkLLMPoT evaluates the committed checkpoint through a loss-function-style objective. This is a feasible measure of training outcomes, because the loss a checkpoint attains on held-out data from the target distribution is the standard way to judge whether pretraining or fine-tuning achieved its goal (【46†Gururangan et al., 2020】). To avoid the trainer overfitting the evaluation, we specified a data commitment protocol.\nL64: The auditor specifies the objective, the trainer freezes the corresponding circuit and commits the checkpoint, and the auditor then chooses the evaluation sequences. The trainer is not able to modify her weights after seeing the commitment.\nL65: Together, these techniques let zkLLMPoT generate and verify proofs quickly, with proving times of $41$–$59$ s for the covered operators on $1.1$–$1.5$B-parameter models and $131$ s at $13$B, and verification below half a second at sequence length $512$, while the commit-then-challenge order removes the trainer’s opportunity to overfit the checkpoint to the auditor’s sequences.\nL66: In summary, our contributions are:\nL67: \nL68:   * •\nL69: \nL70: An outcome-attestation formulation that audits properties attributed to LLM training through a loss-function-style evaluation of committed weights, excluding the backward pass and optimizer from the proved relation;\nL71: \nL72:   * •\nL73: \nL74: A commit-then-challenge protocol in which the trainer binds the circuit and checkpoint before the auditor selects the evaluation sequences, which then enter the statement as public inputs;\nL75: \nL76:   * •\nL77: Operator-level optimizations and fusion for compiling Transformer layers into compact ZK circuits, plus an interface for auditor-specified terminal objectives;\nL78: \nL79:   * •\nL80: \nL81: A component-based study across four model families, yielding proving times of $41$–$59$ s for the covered operators at the $1.x$B scale and $131$ s at $13$B, with verification below half a second.\nL82: ## 2 Preliminaries\nL83: \nL84: In this section, we introduce the properties a zero-knowledge protocol is required to satisfy, and the threat model of our method. We first recall completeness, soundness, zero knowledge, and succinctness for the evaluation relation in section 【6†2.1】. We then state the two-party threat model in section 【7†2.2】.\nL85: ### 2.1 Zero Knowledge Proof\nL86: A zero-knowledge succinct non-interactive argument of knowledge (zk-SNARK) lets a prover convince an auditor that it knows a witness $w$ for a public statement $x$ of an NP relation $R$, without revealing $w$ and without the auditor re-executing the computation (【47†Goldwasser et al., 1989】; 【48†Bitansky et al., 2012】; 【49†Groth, 2016】). Here $R$ is a set of pairs $(x,w)$, and its associated language $L_{R}=\\{\\,x\\mid\\exists\\,w:(x,w)\\in R\\,\\}$ is the set of statements that admit a witness.\nL87: We instantiate $R$ with the definition of model training: the public input is the declared architecture $\\mathcal{A}$, the objective, the evaluation sequences $S$, a commitment to the released weights $\\theta$, and a claimed scalar $v$; the witness is $\\theta$ and the intermediate activations, with a reference checkpoint added when the statement compares two model states.\nL88: An accepting proof convinces the auditor that $v$ is the value the stated objective, such as the output of a loss function, takes on $f_{\\mathcal{A},\\theta}(S)$. We require the following properties for the evaluation relation above.\nL89:   * •\nL90: \nL91: Completeness. If the committed weights, public sequences, activations, and objective value satisfy the declared forward relation, then an honest prover can produce a proof that an honest auditor accepts.\nL92: \nL93:   * •\nL94: Soundness. For every (efficient) malicious prover $\\mathcal{P}^{*}$ and every statement $x\\notin L_{R}$, the verifier accepts $\\mathcal{P}^{*}$’s proof with only negligible probability, which means a trainer cannot substitute uncommitted weights, change the declared architecture or objective, or claim a value $v$ that the committed $\\theta$ does not attain on the public $S$.\nL95: \nL96:   * •\nL97: Zero knowledge. The proof reveals nothing about the witness beyond the truth of the statement (【47†Goldwasser et al., 1989】). This means the auditor learns that the committed $\\theta$ satisfies the evaluation relation but learns no additional information about the weights or intermediate activations.\nL98: \nL99:   * •\nL100: Succinctness. Proof size and verification time grow more slowly than the computation represented by the relation (【48†Bitansky et al., 2012】; 【49†Groth, 2016】). Succinctness makes an outcome certificate transferable because downstream users can verify it without matching the prover’s GPU budget.\nL101: ### 2.2 Threat Model\nL102: \nL103: We use the two-party layout of zkPoT (【44†Garg et al., 2023】). A trainer holds a checkpoint $\\theta$ and any private training corpus as intellectual property. An auditor knows the public architecture $\\mathcal{A}$ and the objective $\\mathcal{L}$, and later chooses the evaluation sequences $S$. Both parties are computationally bounded.\nL104: \nL105:   * •\nL106: Malicious trainer. The trainer need not follow training or the protocol. Its goal is to make the auditor accept a false statement about the committed checkpoint, for example by substituting uncommitted or weaker weights, changing $\\mathcal{A}$ or $\\mathcal{L}$, adapting $\\theta$ after seeing $S$, or claiming a value $v$ that the committed $\\theta$ does not attain on the public $S$. This matches the zkPoT requirement that a false training claim be rejected (【44†Garg et al., 2023】).\nL107: Soundness of section 【6†2.1】 is required against this trainer.\nL108:   * •\nL109: \nL110: Semi-honest auditor. As in zkPoT (【44†Garg et al., 2023】), the auditor follows the verification procedure and reports accept or reject correctly, but may try to recover $\\theta$, intermediate activations, or the private training corpus from the transcript. Zero knowledge is required against this auditor, so the proof reveals only the truth of $v=\\mathcal{L}(f_{\\mathcal{A},\\theta}(S))$.\nL111: Unlike Garg et al.’s zkPoT statement, which asserts that a committed model was obtained by running a declared learning algorithm on a committed dataset (【44†Garg et al., 2023】), our relation encodes only the committed $\\theta$ satisfy the loss function defined by the auditor. The protocol therefore binds the published checkpoint to the auditor’s sequences and loss function choices and does not bind full backpropagation.\nL112: ## 3 zkLLMPoT\nL113: In this section, we detail zkLLMPoT, our protocol for certifying an auditor-chosen evaluation of a committed LLM checkpoint. We first formulate a unified decoder-only training model and the outcome claim in section 【9†3.1】. We then describe the protocol components, including polynomial commitments, linear and lookup gadgets, the commit-then-challenge order, and auditor-specified objectives, in section 【10†3.2】.\nL114: We finally combine these components into the end-to-end protocol and state its soundness in section 【11†3.3】.\nL115: ### 3.1 Formulation\nL116: Contemporary language models such as OPT (【50†Zhang et al., 2022】), Llama (【51†Touvron et al., 2023】), Qwen2.5 (【52†Bai et al., 2023】), and DeepSeek-Coder (【53†DeepSeek-AI, 2024】) are decoder-only Transformers. They differ in normalization, positional encoding, attention layout, and feed-forward activation, but they share one computational skeleton and one training objective.\nL117: We therefore treat them as instances of a single architecture family $\\mathcal{A}$, rather than writing a separate training model for each.\nL118: Forward computation. Fix a vocabulary $\\mathcal{V}$ and a token sequence $x=(x_{1},\\ldots,x_{n})\\in\\mathcal{V}^{n}$. A model in $\\mathcal{A}$ is specified by weights $\\theta$ together with a small operator choice $(\\mathrm{Norm},\\mathrm{Pos},\\mathrm{Attn},\\Phi)$, and computes\nL119: \nL120:  | $$h^{(0)}=\\mathrm{Embed}_{\\theta}(x),\\qquad h^{(\\ell+1)}=\\mathrm{Block}_{\\theta}^{(\\ell)}(h^{(\\ell)}),\\qquad z=h^{(L)}W_{\\mathrm{out}}.$$  |  | (1)\nL121: \nL122: Every block uses the pre-norm residual form common to all of the above families,\nL123:  | $$u=h+\\mathrm{Attn}_{\\theta}\\bigl(\\mathrm{Norm}(h);\\mathrm{Pos}\\bigr),\\qquad h^{\\prime}=u+\\mathrm{FFN}_{\\theta}^{\\Phi}\\bigl(\\mathrm{Norm}(u)\\bigr).$$  |  | (2)\nL124: Here $\\mathrm{Norm}$ is LayerNorm (OPT) or RMSNorm (Llama, Qwen2.5, DeepSeek-Coder); $\\mathrm{Pos}$ is either an additive absolute embedding or rotary embedding (RoPE) inside attention; $\\mathrm{Attn}$ is multi-head, grouped-query, or multi-head latent attention; and $\\Phi$ specializes the feed-forward network (FFN) to ReLU (OPT) or SwiGLU (Llama, Qwen2.5, DeepSeek-Coder). Row-wise softmax turns the logits $z$ into next-token distributions $p_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid x_{\\leq i})=\\mathrm{softmax}(z_{i})$.\nL125: Training objective and updates. Pre-training and supervised fine-tuning of all these models minimize a masked next-token negative log-likelihood. Given a sequence $x$ and a binary mask $m\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n}$ that marks which tokens are predicted,\nL126: \nL127:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{LM}}(\\theta;x,m)\\;=\\;-\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}m_{i+1}\\,\\log p_{\\theta}(x_{i+1}\\mid x_{\\leq i}).$$  |  | (3)\nL128: Causal pre-training takes $m\\equiv 1$ (except the first token); instruction or SFT training zeros $m$ on the prompt and keeps it on the response. The same loss therefore covers OPT-style continued pre-training and Llama / Qwen2.5 / DeepSeek-Coder-style supervised fine-tuning.\nL129: A training run is the first-order iteration $\\theta_{t+1}=\\mathsf{Opt}(\\theta_{t},\\nabla_{\\theta}\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{LM}}(\\theta_{t};x_{t},m_{t}),\\eta_{t},s_{t})$, starting from an initialization $\\theta_{0}$ and releasing a checkpoint $\\theta_{T}$. Here $\\mathsf{Opt}$ is a standard adaptive optimizer (e.g., AdamW) with learning rate $\\eta_{t}$ and state $s_{t}$.\nL130: Preference alignment (PPO, DPO, GRPO) only replaces $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{LM}}$ by another differentiable scalar on one or two forwards of the same map equation 【54†1】; the Transformer itself is unchanged.\nL131: Our protocol zkLLMPoT confidentially and efficiently certifies an evaluation outcome attributed to training or fine-tuning an open decoder-only LLM, without replaying $\\mathsf{Opt}$ or inspecting $\\theta$. zkLLMPoT compiles this model into a zk-SNARK certificate). Writing $f_{\\mathcal{A},\\theta}$ for the forward map and $\\mathcal{L}$ for a scalar objective, the claim is\nL132: \nL133:  | $$v\\;=\\;\\mathcal{L}\\bigl(f_{\\mathcal{A},\\theta}(S)\\bigr).$$  |  | (4)\nL134: The trainer and auditor agree on $\\mathcal{A}$, and the protocol proves that the committed weights $\\theta$ and auditor-chosen sequences $S$ produce the claimed value $v$. zkLLMPoT has four components: polynomial commitments that bind the witness tables, constraint gadgets for the Transformer forward pass, a commit-then-challenge order for auditor-chosen data, and an auditor-chosen scalar objective. We describe each component first and then combine them into the end-to-end protocol.\nL135: Algorithm 1 zkLLMPoT main protocol\nL136: \nL137: 1: Architecture $\\mathcal{A}$; commitment scheme $\\{\\mathsf{Commit},\\mathsf{Open}\\}$ over $\\mathbb{F}_{p}$.\nL138: \nL139: 2: The auditor accepts or rejects $v=\\mathcal{L}\\bigl(f_{\\mathcal{A},\\theta}(S)\\bigr)$ for the released $\\theta$.\nL140: \nL141: 3: The auditor fixes $\\mathcal{L}$ and an acceptance band $[\\underline{v},\\overline{v}]$. The trainer compiles $(\\mathcal{A},\\mathcal{L})$ into $C_{\\mathcal{A},\\mathcal{L}}$.\nL142: 4: The trainer sends $[[\\theta]]\\leftarrow\\mathsf{Commit}(\\theta)$, binding the weights before the auditor’s challenge set $S$ is revealed.\nL143: \nL144: 5: The auditor chooses $S$ and sends it to the trainer.\nL145: \nL146: 6: The trainer evaluates $v\\leftarrow C_{\\mathcal{A},\\mathcal{L}}(\\theta,S)$ and sends $[[v]]$, together with commitments $[[H]]$ to the intermediate activations $h^{(0)},\\dots,h^{(L)}$ of equation 【54†1】 that the layer arguments open. $S$ remains a public input.\nL147: 7: For $\\ell=L,\\dots,1$ the trainer reduces the output claim $h^{(\\ell)}(u_{\\ell})=y_{\\ell}$ through the gadget of $\\mathrm{Block}^{(\\ell)}$ in equation 【55†2】 to an input claim $h^{(\\ell-1)}(u_{\\ell-1})=y_{\\ell-1}$ and a weight claim $\\theta_{\\ell}(w_{\\ell})=c_{\\ell}$, sending each sumcheck transcript.\nL148: \nL149: 8: The auditor replays every reduction and checks the chain: the input claim $\\bigl(u_{\\ell-1},y_{\\ell-1}\\bigr)$ emitted at layer $\\ell$ is the output claim it verifies at layer $\\ell-1$.\nL150: 9: The auditor checks $\\mathsf{Open}$ for $[[\\theta]]$ and $[[H]]$ at the challenge points, evaluates leftover claims on the public table $S$ itself, and recomputes each public table $T\\colon\\{0,1\\}^{n}\\to\\mathbb{F}_{p}$ of the nonlinear gadgets instead of accepting an opening of $T$.\nL151: \nL152: 10: The auditor accepts iff every check passes and $v\\in[\\underline{v},\\overline{v}]$.\nL153: ### 3.2 Protocol Components\nL154: Commitment design. We compile equation 【54†1】–equation 【56†3】 into polynomial constraints over $\\mathbb{F}_{p}$, giving the public circuit $C_{\\mathcal{A},\\mathcal{L}}$ whose description or digest is fixed before the challenge rather than committed with the witness. The witness fills that circuit: $W=(\\theta,H,v)$, the weights, the layer activations $H=(h^{(0)},\\ldots,h^{(L)})$, and the claimed scalar.\nL155: The sequences $S$ are a public input: both parties know them after the auditor sends them, and leftover claims about $S$ are evaluated on that public table. The trainer sends $[[\\theta]]$, $[[H]]$, and $[[v]]$ under a polynomial commitment scheme (【57†Thaler, 2022】). Each tensor is a length-$2^{n}$ table over $\\mathbb{F}_{p}$ identified with its unique multilinear extension, so $[[T]]$ commits to that polynomial rather than to an unstructured string, and proving work is linear in the table size.\nL156: Weight roots are computed once per checkpoint and reused; activation roots are computed per audit. The polynomial commitment scheme is detailed in section 【25†B.3】, and the concrete definition of each operator and its constraint gadget is given in section 【27†B.5】.\nL157: Constraint design over $\\mathbb{F}_{p}$. Tensors in equation 【54†1】 through equation 【56†3】 become tables $T\\colon\\{0,1\\}^{n}\\to\\mathbb{F}_{p}$ by signed fixed-point quantization at a public scale $s$, reduction modulo $p$, and padding to length $2^{n}$. We then compile $f_{\\mathcal{A},\\theta}$ by the standard split of a neural network into linear and nonlinear layers (【36†Liu et al., 2021】; 【37†Weng et al., 2021】), giving each class a constraint gadget.\nL158: The embedding selection, the projections $W_{q},W_{k},W_{v}$, the attention products $QK^{\\top}$ and $PV$, the FFN maps, $W_{\\mathrm{out}}$, and the residual additions in equation 【55†2】 admit low-degree constraints over $\\mathbb{F}_{p}$. We prove the matrix products with hypercube inner-product sumchecks and use direct algebraic constraints for additions and pointwise products, all against committed weight and activation tables.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn3view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/a16z/jolt/blob/main/LICENSE\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/a16z/jolt/blob/main/LICENSE: Error fetching--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nperf: booleanity memory optimization [JOLT-206] by 0xAndoroid · Pull Request #936 · a16z/jolt · GitHub (https://github.com/a16z/jolt/pull/936)\nciteturn3view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view1\",\"id\":102}); Total lines: 322\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFeat/batched sumcheck mem by 0xAndoroid · Pull Request #916 · a16z/jolt · GitHub (https://github.com/a16z/jolt/pull/916)\nciteturn3view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view1\",\"id\":93}); Total lines: 242\n---------------------------------------------------------------